  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =7$ and $\\sigma =2.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 4.62) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{4.62-7}{2}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge -1.19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14263116/assets/f96faea70d10478e9ac5fca7f34e76a0b592ddb87dd5749a93807687907f92bc.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.19) & =1-P(Z<-1.19) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -1.19) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.19)=0.1170.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.19) & =1-Φ(-1.19) \\\\  & =1-0.1170 \\\\  & =0.8830.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 4.62)=0.8830.\n$$",
